<h2>What Amlodipine Actually Costs at the Pharmacy</h2>
<p>Amlodipine is a calcium channel blocker prescribed for high blood pressure and certain types of chest pain (angina). It has been available as a generic since 2007 when Pfizer's patent on the brand-name version, Norvasc, expired. Since then, dozens of manufacturers have entered the market, pushing prices down to pennies per pill at the wholesale level.</p>
<p>If you walk into a pharmacy without insurance and ask for a 30-day supply of generic amlodipine, you can expect to pay anywhere from $3 to $45 depending on the pharmacy, your dosage, and whether you use a discount card. The most common dosages are 2.5 mg, 5 mg, and 10 mg, and the price difference between them is usually negligible since the manufacturing cost is essentially the same regardless of strength.</p>
<p>At the low end, discount card programs from GoodRx, SingleCare, and RxSaver routinely bring the price to $3 to $6 for a 30-day supply at major chain pharmacies. At Walmart and other retailers with $4 generic programs, amlodipine is typically listed at $9 for a 30-day supply and $24 for a 90-day supply. Costco's pharmacy, which does not require a membership in most states, also offers competitive pricing in the $4 to $8 range.</p>
<p>At the high end, walking into CVS or Walgreens without any discount card and paying the retail cash price can run $30 to $45 for the same 30-day supply. That markup is not because the medication is expensive to produce — it is the pharmacy's retail pricing, and it is exactly the kind of gap that makes price comparison essential.</p>

