<h2>Generic Amlodipine vs. Brand-Name Norvasc</h2>
<p>Amlodipine was originally sold as Norvasc by Pfizer, and the brand name is still technically available. However, there is almost never a reason to fill the brand version. Generic amlodipine contains the same active ingredient, in the same dosage, and meets the same FDA standards for quality, purity, and bioavailability.</p>
<p>The cost difference is enormous. Brand-name Norvasc can run $400 or more for a 30-day supply without insurance, compared to under $10 for the generic. Unless your doctor has specifically written "dispense as written" on the prescription — which is extremely rare for amlodipine — your pharmacist will automatically substitute the generic. If you ever see a surprisingly high price at the register, the first thing to ask is whether you are getting the generic version.</p>

