<h2>Insurance Copays vs. Cash Price</h2>
<p>Here is something worth knowing: your insurance copay for amlodipine might actually be higher than the cash price with a discount card. Many insurance plans place generics on Tier 1 with copays of $5 to $15. If a discount card gets you the same medication for $3, paying cash is the better deal.</p>
<p>The one consideration is your deductible. If you are trying to meet your annual deductible or out-of-pocket maximum, running prescriptions through insurance counts toward those thresholds, while cash payments typically do not. For a medication this inexpensive, the difference is usually not significant, but it is worth thinking about if you have other costly prescriptions during the year.</p>

